{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-\u00e6on/1472135-tc-aeon-actual-play-desperate-stars-1","id":1472135,"title":"[TC: Aeon Actual Play] Desperate Stars #1","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum","Trinity Continuum: \u00c6on"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-01-09T10:12:38-08:00","id":1472135,"title":"[TC: Aeon Actual Play] Desperate Stars #1","content":"Greetings, everyone!<br /> <br /> As I've mentioned before, I've been a fan of Trinity every since the limited edition core book dropped way back in 1997. I never really had a chance to play it, but I still collected, read, and re-read every book in the game line. I fell in love with the multitude of ideas and possibilities the game presented, and always regretted that I couldn't find a group that showed any interest.<br /> <br /> All of that changed a few weeks ago when I convinced by current group to let me run them through a campaign for the new edition. I turned into a bit of a maniac, writing up two different campaigns for them to choose from, as well as various plot hooks, plot points, alien creatures, enemy spies... You get the idea.<br /> <br /> Finally, after interruptions to our regular gaming schedule due to the holidays, family commitments and Covid scares, we got to play our first real session last night. Being the over-excited nerd that I am, I couldn't wait to post a real play write-up of the session.<br /> <br /> This is my first time attempting a real play thread, so apologies to any mistakes, omissions, gaps in continuity, or lack of common sense that I display as a GM and as as a conveyer of these events. I make no such claims on behalf of my players.<br /> <br /> I'll start off with our actual first session, the Session 0 we had a few weeks ago where I walked them through character creation. I laid out the basics of the setting and its history, and gave them a choice of two campaign styles: cyberpunk or space opera. The group quickly settled on space opera, which I pitched as a mix of Stargate: SG1 and Star Trek. Then, the characters were made based on what would be the needs of the campaign and the group's desires.<br /> <br /> So, here are the PCs:<br /> <br /> <b>Andromeda &quot;Andy&quot; Hassan</b> - A Upeo Wa Macho teleporter and pilot/physicist with daddy issues.<br /> <br /> <b>Giselle</b> - A wandering clairvoyant detective. Her family was killed by an Aberrant as they attempted to flee the destruction of Paris. She now seeks justice for them.<br /> <br /> <b>Radian Moss</b> - A psychokinetic ex-Orgotek operative who now works on a freelance basis for Aeon and the other orders. He did some sketchy things for Orgotek, and does not have fond memories of his time there.<br /> <br /> <b>Miguel Ramirez</b> - An electrokinetic biotech developer who works primarily as a researcher for Aeon.<br /> <br /> <b>Dr. Xin Wu</b> - A vitakinetic doctor who has had extensive experience in Doctors Without Borders. Is also secretly a Ministry spy.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: Andy's player actually read the book ahead of time (shock!) and developed a PC that's roughly a mix of Anne McCaffery's The Rowan and Samantha Carter from Stargate SG 1. Giselle and Radian Moss's (who just goes by Moss) players picked fairly straightforward concepts from the ISRA and Orgotek sections of the book. While this edition is supposed to be more optimistic than 1st ed, I couldn't resist the opportunity to use Moss as an ex-Option 8 operative. Xin's player went for a fairly tricky concept, but we worked together heavily and I'm excited to see how his personal plotline develops. Ramirez's player was swamped with work - Denver finally got its first snows of the season and he drives a plow truck - and was too exhausted to think during Session 0, much less make a character. He told me, &quot;You know what I like to play. Just make the group's mechanic.&quot; So, here we are.<br /> <br /> Next Post: The actual first session! Training Day.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25998-loneshark","name":"Loneshark","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-09T11:13:30-08:00","id":1472138,"title":"","content":"Desperate Stars Session 1: Operation Apophis<br /> <br /> The scene opens with the PCs having spent the past three months, fresh out of the Prometheus Chamber, in a special Aeon-sponsored training facility on Luna. They have been put through rigorous physical and noetic drills by Lt. Munroe of the Legions and Dr. McCoy of Aeon. Thus far, the PCs have superficially gotten to know one another, and they are aware that they are being prepared for a special joint mission sponsored by the Psi Orders and Aeon, but they don't know exactly what that is, yet. They do know that they have been given extensive access to reports on both the Coalition and the Chromatics, which they pursued thoroughly as a group.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: This gave me a chance to dump as much of the in-canon backstory on both the Coalition and Chromatics as Aeon is aware of. The PCs are aware that the Coalition is vaguely &quot;up to no good,&quot; and the Chromatics are psychotically devoted to killing as many humans as possible.<br /> <br /> Dr. Xin is extremely curious about why they have been allowed access to this exact information, and how it relates to their future mission. He surreptitiously observed Dr. McCoy comparing the PCs' physiological and noetic profiles against what parallel information Aeon possesses of the Chromatics. Xin guesses that their future mission will have something to do with the aliens, and so he takes it upon himself to start researching them as much as he can. He decides to consult with Miguel on methods to counteract the Chromatics' abilities.<br /> <br /> Miguel is aware of psionic restraint collars, and theorizes that there might be a way to miniaturize them so that they can be delivered via conventional weaponry. But, first, they have to get one so that he can reverse engineer it. Xin taps his patron, a man high in the command structure of the Ministry, who secures one and has it discreetly delivered to the team.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: Xin has a level 3 patron. This &quot;favor&quot; was highly unusual and is definitely going to come up again later. But, on the other hand, player-initiated intrigue! I had to do nothing! I love it!<br /> <br /> These actions do not go unnoticed by the rest of the team, despite Xin's best efforts. They have a group conversation about this. Giselle is reticent about the idea, because anything that could be used against another psionic race could easily be used against Earth's psions. But, she knows that they will go ahead with it anyway, so chooses to simply observe their progress for now. Andy, on the other hand, takes a more direct approach, and physically threatens Miguel. She makes it clear that any screw-ups will fall squarely on him. Moss just plays on his minicomp. With that said, Miguel moves ahead with his project, and successfully reverse engineers the psi-restraint collar and beings his plans for miniaturization.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: Be me, scrambling through the super-science rules in the corebook mid-game while the PCs argue amongst themselves. This is the exact sort of stuff I made Miguel's character to do, though, and the son of a bitch never fails a roll, so he successfully completes the first milestone.<br /> <br /> All of this occurs right under the noses of their trainers. Once the inter-party drama concludes, the PCs move into one of their final training sessions. They know that they are nearing the end of their program, and eager to find out what their future holds for them. Suddenly, mid-training, there is a full base alert. Aberrants are attacking. The team wants to help defend the base, but Lt. Munroe tells them that the Legions, Orgotek, and Proteus are already scrambling their squads to handle it. They're not ready yet, and she orders them to join the rest of the civilians in the base at the emergency bunker 3 sub-levels down.<br /> <br /> That's when the doors to the training hall burst open and sub-Aberrant mutants rush in at them. The group's first taste of combat is swift and hectic. Moss opens with a flash of fire, and it ends with Andy cutting down half of the mutants with her sonic blade.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: Heretofore I just shorten it to &quot;mutants&quot;. Saves a lot of time. I made these enemies minor characters, one-hit dropped. As a group we're still getting used to Initiative slots and purchasing stunts, so I will slowly and gradually up the difficulty of combat as the game progresses.<br /> <br /> The PCs experience a brief moment of panic--Andy\u2019s first instinct is to find a ship and get the hell away from here. Moss also wants to find a ship and blast some Aberrants. Giselle, Miguel, and Xin want to head to the base\u2019s emergency bunker, as Lt. Munroe ordered. Giselle uses her psychometry on one of the mutants and learns that it was transported here in a pod with several other mutants and at least one \u201cblack spot,\u201d a source of significant Corruption.<br /> <br /> They turned to Lt. Munroe for guidance, and then the roof of the training room caved in. Both the team and their trainers survive, and through their minicomps Lt. Munroe tasks them with finding any other survivors they can, and getting down to the bunker.<br /> <br /> Miguel makes creative use of his powers to access the base's OpNet and uses the survivors' minicomps to locate them and find the safest route to the bunker. The team sets off. They find a couple of groups of survivors easily, and begin to debate what to do after they\u2019ve found them all. Their debate is cut short when a Divergent Aberrant and his mutant cronies round the corner of the corridor.<br /> <br /> Another brief skirmish breaks out. After a lackluster performance with his Thermokinesis, Moss elects to rely on his laser pistol. His beam pins down the mutants, until the Aberrant uses its powers to short out the pistol. Giselle and Miguel fire their own weapons and take out the mutants, while Andy opens a portal next to the Aberrant and hits it with her sonic blade. Xin takes advantage of the now open path and strides up to the Aberrant. After successfully activating Assault (GM Notes: 9 friggin successes!), he slices his bio-claws into the Aberrant\u2019s chest, severing several vital organs in one blow that instantly slays it.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: The group displayed a little more coordination here, and after a little bit of fumbling learned some effective tricks. This is also when I discovered that Moss's player had purchased a Spider Combat Harness during character creation without realizing what an effective armor/weapon combo it is.<br /> <br /> Giselle again uses her powers on the downed Aberrant, and learns that the pod it came in as just one of several that the Aberrants warped to Luna from aboard a Coalition ship. They are the first psions to discover that the Coalition and the Aberrants are collaborating.<br /> <br /> There is another debate about what to do--get the survivors to safety, leave them to join the assault, both or neither. The group eventually opts to follow Lt. Munroe\u2019s original orders. They pick up all of the remaining survivors, and the scenario ends.<br /> <br /> After the team turns in their reports, they are commended by Lt. Munroe for their actions, and informed that their training has ended. They are introduced to Cpt. Everett Thomas, who will be leading a deep space exploration mission aboard the Leviathan ship Askani. Aeon and the Psi Orders suspected that the Coalition had some involvement with Aberrants, and Giselle's visions confirm it.<br /> <br /> The team is given their next assignment. They will be crewing a state-of-the-art ship that will join the Askani on its quest to find technologies and allies that will aid them against Earth\u2019s various enemies.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: I intentionally made this scenario short and hectic, to ease my players into this new system and get them used to its concepts. All of the roleplay at the beginning was totally initiated by them and took up the bulk of the session, and reminded me how lucky I am to be a part of this group. I had written down a couple of possible Complications, but forgot to make good use of them. It's something that I'll have to be conscientious of next time.<br /> <br /> Next Week -- Mars! Intrigue! A brand new ship! This week can't go by fast enough.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25998,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25998-loneshark","user":"Loneshark","date":". 01-09-2022, 02:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25998-loneshark","name":"Loneshark","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-09T13:09:13-08:00","id":1472142,"title":"","content":"Thanks for posting. Looking forward to seeing this develop.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:georgia\">Organizer for the Dead Gamers Society: The So Cal World of Darkness Meetup. </span><a href=\"http://www.meetup.com/Dead-Gamers-Society/\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.meetup.com/Dead-Gamers-Society/</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/85-drumheller","name":"Drumheller","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar85_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-22T15:42:22-08:00","id":1473047,"title":"","content":"Okay, so last week didn't happen. Neither will this week. Half my players had family stuff and other obligations, so, it's understandable. Frustrating for an over-excited SG, but understandable.<br /> <br /> But! Since Prometheus Unbound dropped this week (FINALLY!!!!) I now have full Manta stats! Andy's player spotted that plot hook in the corebook during our Session 0, and over the weeks they convinced me to use it. Now I can! I mean, I was just going to write up my own stats, but this gives me a more solid base to work from. I'll probably drop the Sluggish tag and either reduce the Cargo rating by 1 or increase the cost to Legendary--this is meant to be a plot element for my game, not something the players can just grab by spending X points. I also wanted to add a couple of other bells &amp; whistles that'll make it fun for the group. I'll detail that more after next week's session.<br /> <br /> It should happen. Really. I swear. I hope.<br /> <br /> And a special mention to Leath Sheales for the Bioapp Control CC. This is exactly what I wanted Miguel's character to accomplish. Thank you for giving me something to work with. The less prep a SG needs, the better!<br /> <br /> Keep an eye on this thread, because it's not dead yet!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25998-loneshark","name":"Loneshark","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-22T23:42:42-08:00","id":1473060,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Loneshark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-\u00e6on/1472135-tc-aeon-actual-play-desperate-stars-1?p=1473047#post1473047\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And a special mention to Leath Sheales for the Bioapp Control CC. This is exactly what I wanted Miguel's character to accomplish. Thank you for giving me something to work with. The less prep a SG needs, the better!</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm very glad it's timely and helpful for you!<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Writer. Developer.</i> <b>World of Darkness</b> | <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> | <b>The Trinity Continuum</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/156-bunyip","name":"Bunyip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1880"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-01-31T10:13:08-08:00","id":1473681,"title":"","content":"Desperate Stars #2: Operation Osiris<br /> <br /> Six months have passed since the team graduated from their training on Luna. They have been training with their new ship, the Manta-class <i>Dayspring</i>. The Dayspring has been refitted so that it possesses some minor differences from its sisters, giving it greater maneuverability at the cost of requiring separate formatting for the ship itself and its Ares pod. Sam has been named the pilot, and Giselle the navigator. Mason* controls the weaponry, while Miguel handles engineering. Xin remains the emergency medical officer (and surreptitiously sets up personal security for all medical files he has gathered, and a secure method of communication with his Patron in the Ministry).**<br /> <br /> *GM Notes: Sam's player had a conflicting commitment, and so she wasn't able to attend this session. It's part of the reason we made her character the pilot--so that if she has to miss anything, we simply say that her character has to stay aboard the Dayspring as pilot. After a repeated goof on my part where I kept calling Moss the wrong name, the players decided that Mason was actually better, so the PC's name was changed retroactively.<br /> <br /> **GM Notes: He successfully made his Cunning + Tech roll to do this, but wasn't able to pay off the Complication. What was the result? *Insert Evil GM Laughter Here* We'll get to that later. <br /> <br /> They've been training at a small Aeon/Ministry facility on Mars, where security has been tight and everything is tense as the team gets ready to start official missions with the Askani and its crew. The team has been living on the ship during that time, and Miguel has been taking time to get to know the ship's Tesser, which also self-identifies as Dayspring. He asks a few questions to get an idea of what its like to be a Tesser, including its opinion of the Askani. The Dayspring has only been in contact with her through intermittent radio transmissions, relaying mission information and updates strictly as necessary, so it hasn't had time to form one. But, it did assure Miguel that the Askani loves and has full confidence in her crew.<br /> <br /> As the group is wrapping up a routine flight-training mission, it pulls into the facility's hangar bay just in time to register an explosion in the facility's small civilian sector. Facility security scrambles--the crew is ordered to stay aboard the Dayspring while the bulk of security rushes to the sight of the blast, leaving only a couple of guards watching the hangar. The crew, being PCs and not the kind of players to sit on the sidelines, use the ship's sensors to try and get more information. The Dayspring is equipped with an aberscope, which detects minute traces of Corruption both at the blast sight and heading straight for the hangar bay!<br /> <br /> At that moment, an armed mob storms the hangar bay and kills the guards. They fire wildly at the ship (which does little more than annoy it), while their leader rushes at the Dayspring with a strange device in his hands.<br /> <br /> Xin, overly confident in his abilities, exits the ship to deal with the attackers personally. The first couple of shots convince him to find some cover. Meanwhile, Miguel, Giselle, and Mason come up with a crazy idea--they harmonize their noetic fields to channel Miguel's electromagnetic discharge through the Dayspring's weaponry and knock out as many of the attackers at once as possible*. It works, and the Dayspring's laser carbines start spitting bolts of electric pain at the attackers, taking out the bulk of them with a couple of shots. The attackers' leader avoids the bolts and gets close to the ship. That is when Xin leaps out from cover and easily incapacitates him. The device falls out of his hands and clatters to the ground.<br /> <br /> *GM Notes: So, I gushed to my players about Prometheus Unbound before the game. But, I was glad I did, because the creative mode rules served as an excellent guideline here. It was a crazy idea, but I let it ride, and the players got enough successes to pull it off.<br /> <br /> With the security detail occupied, the PCs are left to handle the aftermath. The attackers are sufficiently stunned that they won't be getting up anytime soon. Xin wants to take the attackers' leader, who is registering significant Corruption, to the ship's medical bay for questioning. Giselle doesn't want him anywhere near the ship, and the Dayspring agrees, going so far as to shut its boarding hatch in protest. Xin relents, and he and Mason find an unused maintenance bay to drag the man to.<br /> <br /> Miguel picks up the device the man had tried to attach to the ship, and takes some time to study it. With a cursory examination he's able to deduce that this thing is both of alien origin, and has been significantly modified, most likely by someone with an Aberrant's level of genius. Giselle uses her psychometry on it to find that it was given to their attackers by a woman named Madison Short, who is hiding somewhere in the colony, and they are all part of an Aberrant cult.<br /> <br /> Xin and Mason, meanwhile, have secured the ringleader in the seclusion of the maintenance bay. They use their minicomps to identify him as Kole Silver, a Polish national who is registered as a sanitation worker in the colony. Xin doses him with an impromptu drug cocktail that acts as a truth serum. Kole, in his inebriated state, confirms that he's a member of an Aberrant cult. Madison gave him the device to attach to the ship at any cost. He doesn't know exactly how to use the device or what it was supposed to do, but he was taught how to activate it. He tells them how to find Madison, and that she relayed orders to him and the rest of the cult inside the colony from somewhere outside, where their Aberrant master waited.<br /> <br /> Xin makes note of all of this. He and Mason decide that Kole's knowledge of the device makes him too dangerous a risk. Mason, whose previous experiences leave him with little compunction about it, executes Kole and uses his powers to incinerate the body.*<br /> <br /> GM Notes: I used this as an opportunity to give Xin and Mason a Level 2 Bond, &quot;Shared Secret.&quot; The execution may come as a shock to some, but not me, considering the players involved. They think they've gotten away with it for now, but someone was watching...<br /> <br /> With this knowledge in hand, it's childishly easy for the team to locate Madison Short, and colony logs track her comings and goings. Madison would regularly take a rover out to the Martian surface, and after several kilometers its tracking data would blip out, only to reappear hours later when it returned to the colony. Miguel informs Aeon, and Xin informs the Ministry. The team is given leave to track Madison and see where she is going. Aeon takes possession of the device to bury it in the deepest hole they can find, and then throw that hole into another hole.<br /> <br /> Thanks to her previous use of psychometry, Giselle is able to use Dowsing to trail Madison from a safe distance without the team being spotted. She and Mason follow in a rover, while the rest of the team waits aboard the Dayspring, ready to launch and meet them wherever they end up.<br /> <br /> Giselle senses that they are getting close to finding the Aberrant. Madison's trail ends at an old, abandoned mining facility. A quick search of Martian records reveals that it was one of the earliest ones built on the planet in the early 2060s, just before the Aberrant wars. The perfect place for an Aberrant to hide.<br /> <br /> Giselle and Mason are able to sneak in (again, thanks to Giselle), and travel through the mine. It's important to note here that both took the extra precaution of wearing environment suits. At the center of the mine, they find two Aberrants, and a sizable cult of nearly thirty individuals. They overhear the conversation taking place between the Aberrants: One is berating the other for his failure at planting the device, his ham-fisted manner of carrying out his orders, and exposing the cult. He's ordered to clean up his mess before the second Aberrant warps away.<br /> <br /> The Dayspring has now come within broadcast range of the mine. Miguel hacks the mine's computers and takes control of its atmospheric controls while Xin deploys and joins the rest of the team. The team is safe in their environment suits while Miguel lowers the oxygen in the mine enough to render the cultists unconscious. Without his backup, the Aberrant is easy prey for the team, as Xin and Mason descend upon him. Xin nearly takes a blow*, and delivers an even stronger one back at the Aberrant. Mason finishes it off with laser fire.<br /> <br /> *GM Notes: Seriously, Vitakinetics are tough!<br /> <br /> Miguel reactivates the mine's atmosphere, and Xin uses his iatrosis to rouse the cult. Their leader, a man who identifies himself as Cameron Hodge, orders the rest of the cult to surrender peacefully. Aeon and the Ministry arrive to take them into custody.<br /> <br /> The team is allowed to question Hodge (formally), who is more than happy to tell them everything he knows about the cult and its plans. He doesn't fear them for an instant. He tells them that the device was indeed of alien origin, and one of his Aberrant &quot;gods&quot; turned it into a weapon to cleanse the galaxy of psions and &quot;alien filth.&quot; It was going to generate a quantum warp field around the Dayspring the second it attempted to teleport, and the resulting conflict would tear the ship and everyone aboard it apart at the atomic level. Miguel realizes that this would have created a black hole where the Dayspring had been, swallowing Mars along with it!<br /> <br /> Hodge reveals the names of his gods: Psychomachie (whom they just eliminated), Azimuth, Legba, and Lamia. The other three are on another planet called Duat, where they found the abandoned alien tech, and even now are preparing to be used against the psions. Hodge gives them the star charts necessary to find it. When asked what &quot;alien filth&quot; he's referring to, Hodge names all of the races Humanity has met so far, including some as yet undiscovered.<br /> <br /> Suitably horrified, Aeon gives the PCs their first &quot;official&quot; mission as soon as the Askani arrives. They are to travel to Duat and assess the Aberrant threat there.<br /> <br /> Final GM Notes: Okay, this was a big one, with a lot happening in a fairly short span of time. We're all still getting used to the system--I even tossed out initiative in the first fight because it was just easier to referee it that way. This was also the first time I made use of the Bond rules... which now I have to refamiliarize myself with to make sure that I'm using them correctly. As for the complication that Xin's player activated at the beginning of the game? Well, that will just have to wait for another story...<br /> <br /> Next Session: We finally meet the Askani and her crew! The campaign kicks into high-gear as the team undertakes their first off-world mission! Strange worlds, alien tech, and vicious monsters await!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25998-loneshark","name":"Loneshark","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-12T12:17:05-08:00","id":1474507,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Operation Golden Barque</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">GM Notes: Both Sam\u2019s player and Miguel\u2019s player were absent from this session. At their request, Mason\u2019s player took over for Miguel, and I took over for Sam, who mostly stayed aboard the Dayspring.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A few days after the PCs root out the Aberrant cult on Mars, the Askani arrived in orbit above the planet. The PCs join the main Leviathan, docking their ship Dayspring to it. They are joined by the Essex, a scarab frigate, and the Spiral, a biotech dropship used by the marines aboard the Askani.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">They jump into orbit around the planet tentatively known as Duat, using the star charts that they took from the Aberrant cult. Only Mason experiences a sense of distortion--for the rest of the crew, it took only seconds. A clairsentient scan of the planet reveals that there are roughly three hundred humans on the planet\u2019s surface, including mutants. They can only detect a single, active Aberrant.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The moment the small fleet\u2019s jump ended, however, a stray radio transmission was emitted from the Dayspring. The Dayspring (which Mason insists on calling \u2018Computer\u2019) doesn\u2019t have an explanation for it, and the Askani\u2019s crew only recorded garbled \u2018noise\u2019. Captain Thomas of the Askani is prepared to ignore it, but the crew of the Dayspring isn\u2019t going to let this go, especially when they realize that the transmission was sent from Xin\u2019s personal minicomp.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The crew gathers aboard the Askani for a mission briefing. There, Mason enlists Miguel to help him set up a monitoring system to watch the ship\u2019s chief engineer, a man named Kevin Sydney. Mr. Sydney is still technically on Orgotek\u2019s payroll, and Mason trusts the company as far as he can throw them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">GM Notes: This edition is supposed to be more hopeful, but Mason\u2019s player and I discussed his character\u2019s background and motivations, and he wanted something a little more cyberpunk. I felt that this was the perfect opportunity to work in Option 8, which we made his PC a former member of. My personal head canon incorporates the plot hook revealed about Cassel in Prometheus Unbound, which I\u2019m going to use to justify Option 8\u2019s existence when I get around to introducing the Doyen to the game.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Xin, meanwhile, went straight to his direct superior within the Ministry (and his 3 dot patron) who arranged to be included amongst the Askani\u2019s crew. Xin explains that he believes his equipment has been somehow compromised. His patron agrees, and shows Xin security footage of him and Mason executing the Aberrant cultist Kole Silver on Mars. Xin tries to explain himself by insisting that because Kole\u2019s genetic imprint was the trigger for the weaponized alien device, he was too dangerous to risk being allowed to live. His patron reminds him that he is a member of an order full of telepaths who could have easily wiped the information and the device\u2019s existence from Kole\u2019s mind!</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Xin\u2019s patron reveals that the security footage was sent to him directly from Xin\u2019s minicomp. He takes the minicomp and destroys it immediately. He also informs Xin that he has taken steps to cover up this entire fiasco, including making sure that all records of Kole Silver\u2019s existence were erased. Xin gets the impression that his patron is more annoyed by the fact that Xin didn\u2019t go through official channels than anything. Xin is then informed that unofficially he will have a black mark on his record with the Ministry, and both he and Mason will be watched more closely from now on.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Giselle realizes that there was a minute trace of corruption coming from Xin, but it disappeared when he got rid of his minicomp.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">GM Notes: Xin going to his patron triggered the complication that he rolled last session. Something was obviously influencing his minicomp. When I later told Miguel\u2019s player what happened during the session, he came to the conclusion that Xin may be secretly compromised by Aberrants, even possibly working with them. I said nothing to confirm or deny this. </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">My group is a paranoid bunch. I blame myself. The last game I ran for them was Vampire the Masquerade. I had them terrified of a conspicuously parked, unmarked van.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Finally, the team attends the mission briefing, where they discuss both their objective and how to carry it out. Xin asks why they can\u2019t just bomb the planet from orbit (which the Askani\u2019s tactical officer, Col. Nathan Christopher, agrees with). Cpt Thomas reminds them that the last device salvaged from this planet would have created a planet-swallowing black hole. Who knows what would happen if they blew everything up at once?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It\u2019s decided that the crew of the Dayspring will scout the surface and the cult\u2019s encampment, a small colony near the middle of the planet\u2019s largest continent. The team assumes that, thanks to that strange radio transmission, they are expected. They fly down in the Dayspring, and land it several kilometers from the cult\u2019s encampment. Giselle uses her Dowsing to lead them straight there.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">En route to the camp, they come across several guard patrols. They manage to evade them with little difficulty, again thanks to Giselle (GM Notes: Don\u2019t ignore your Survival skill, kids!). They reach the edge of the camp, where they observe the cult\u2019s setup. The camp is built around a dig site, with several tents lining the perimeter. Cultists turned slaves dig into the dirt and rock at the base of a small cliff, under the watch of brutal guards. At the top of the cliff, priests worship constantly at the shrines of their \u201cgods\u201d.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">While the PCs watch, one of the slaves emerges from a dug-out pit, holding a strange device, and cries that he found something. The rest of the slaves cheer, while two mutants emerge from a nearby cave. They snatch the device from the slave, then kick him back into the hole and growl for everyone to get back to work. They then carry the device back into the cave.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The PCs determine that one of the Aberrants they\u2019re looking for must be in the cave. They formulate a plan to infiltrate the camp and get more information. They double back and ambush one of the guard patrols. The guards are normal humans armed with conventional weapons, and stand little chance against a full team of psions.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Xin uses his Ruin to paralyze the last guard standing after the others mop the forest floor with the rest. They take the guards\u2019 uniforms, and Xin uses his truth serum on the remaining guard, and they question him about the camp. He reveals that two of the Aberrants, Azimuth and Lamia, are off-world, searching for a new base since they lost contact with the Mars branch. Legba remains behind, studying and modifying anything that the slaves dig up. The guards, the priesthood, and the mutants treat the rest of the cult horribly--the slaves were promised a new paradise, and instead are being worked to death.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The PCs leave the guard to fend for himself when the paralysis and the serum wear off, and dress themselves in the uniforms to infiltrate the camp. They slip in easily, as it seems the guards were chosen for their brute strength and not their ability to recognize faces. They ask around (GM Note: Finally using the Intrigue rules!) and learn that none of the slaves and guards are allowed in the caverns, where the \u201cgods\u201d lair. Only the mutants are allowed, and they deliver the gods\u2019 edicts to the priests and guards, who enforce them upon the slaves. Anything dug up that is too dangerous or volatile for Legba to use is stored in a nearby shed.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The PCs decide that they need to get into the cave, and they need a distraction. Xin contacts his patron, who has been telepathically monitoring the situation, and asks him to inform Cpt. Thomas. He confirms that the Askani\u2019s marines will be on standby.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The team sneaks over to the shed labeled \u201cDangerous\u201d by the plot, and sets up an explosive charge. They wait for it to go off--it takes out the entire guards\u2019 barracks with it. Everyone goes scrambling and screaming. Several mutants emerge from the cave and start roaring orders. The PCs have no trouble slipping inside.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Once inside, they find a series of carefully dug out tunnels, revealing bits of discarded technology embedded into the dirt and rock. Up ahead, they hear several mutants arguing about what they should do--leave the planet? Fight? What does Legba say? No one knows.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The PCs also feel a corruption zone up ahead--the workshop of a mad Aberrant.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">GM Notes: We ended here for the night because the rest of the group felt more comfortable taking on an Aberrant and who knows how many mutants with all players present. It took a surprising amount of time to get to this point, as well. What will they find down there? What nasty surprises do I--I mean, does the Aberrant have in store for them? We\u2019ll have to wait to find out.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25998,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25998-loneshark","user":"Loneshark","date":". 02-13-2022, 01:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25998-loneshark","name":"Loneshark","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-04T10:30:56-08:00","id":1475782,"title":"","content":"Welcome back! This write-up is a bit late (it's been a busy week). But here it is, Operation: Golden Barque, Part 2.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: I had ANDY's player read over this thread so that she could get caught up, and she pointed out that I had been using the wrong name for her character the entire time. Whoops! Just... ignore that, will you? Thanks.<br /> <br /> The team, now fully reassembled, managed to sneak into the Aberrant base on the planet Duat. Of the three Aberrants that control the cult, only one was currently on-world. Legba, the very Aberrant who had been weaponizing the alien technology on the planet, was tucked away in his lab.<br /> <br /> The team had called in the Askani's marine contingent, who were currently assaulting the camp up above, taking down the guards and rescuing the slaves.<br /> <br /> The PCs heard mutants arguing up ahead in the tunnels that made up the Aberrant lair. There was growing dissension amongst their ranks, as those loyal to the other Aberrants (Azimuth and Lamia) felt no need to risk their lives on Legba's behalf. Miguel managed to detect the ad-hoc intercom system that the Aberrants had setup in the caves, and used his Technokinesis to hack into it. The team knew what Azimuth's voice sounded like, so Miguel synthesized it and ordered the mutants to dispose of Legba, lest his discoveries fall into the hands of unbelievers. About half of the mutants fell for it, and rushed down the tunnels to take Legba out. The rest fled through side-tunnels, deciding that they had no interest in a war between gods.<br /> <br /> The team heard brief sounds of combat as they followed the mutants' trail through the tunnels. They were led straight to Legba's workshop, where the Aberrant stood victorious over the slaughtered mutants. He coalesced out of the air as a humanoid mass of glassy black nano-machines.<br /> <br /> The team got the jump on him. Mason pinned him down with a pyrokinetic blast, while Xin charged Legba and smacked him with Atrocity. Legba went from Maimed to Taken Out in short order. Miguel, rather than risk this monster getting back up again, fried him down to atoms.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: I swear I am going to get better/smarter with these Aberrant antagonists, I promise.<br /> <br /> With the cult's power broken and the camp secured, the team checked out the remains of the lair. They found the Aberrants' main worship hall, where propaganda posters plastered the walls and luxurious silks, pillows, and food were piled high. They finally got to see what Azimuth and Lamia looked like: A shark-fin headed, perpetually smug jerk, and a four-armed snake-lady trying way too hard to be sexy in a very creepy way, respectively.<br /> <br /> While they explored, Giselle came across a strange and deeply unsettling discovery; a crumpled photograph of a young, sandy-blonde boy with her parents. Except Giselle's parents were dead, and she didn't have a brother! And what the hell was it doing in an Aberrant den?<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Andy felt a strange pull towards one of the alien devices seemingly tossed aside in Legba's workshop. Andy had enough technical expertise to discern that the metallic sphere that drew her attention wasn't anything like the rest of the tech discovered on this world. She pocketed it for further study.<br /> <br /> Once back aboard the Dayspring/Askani, the team was congratulated by Cpt. Thomas on a successful mission, and invited to share drinks with him in the Askani's lounge. While the rest of the team took a moment to relax, Andy received a strange message sent to her minicomp from the Askani--the recording of Xin and Mason executing Silver on Mars. The video is followed by a featureless agent who puts a finger to its vague lips and says, &quot;Shhh!&quot;, and then giggles before winking out.<br /> <br /> Andy does some investigating with the help of the Dayspring--being formatted to the ship makes it much easier for her to navigate its systems. Together they realize that the message, while seemingly genuine, came from someplace aboard the Askani, and the Leviathan is unaware of who or how the message was sent. Andy suspects that someone is trying to manipulate the team to sow division in its ranks, and recruits Miguel who help her investigate once they're aboard the Askani.<br /> <br /> While they get to know some of the Askani's senior crew, including its EXO Neal Shaara, Co-Pilot Sooraya Qadir, and Chief Medical Officer Dr. Nezhno Abidemi, Miguel uses his Technokinesis to link with the Askani's hardtech systems. He senses a quantum presence, and directs Xin to excuse himself and then make his way to a secluded area of the ship. Miguel finds and confronts the entity that's been responsible for all of the strange transmissions and messages bouncing around the ship: The very first Aberrant they ever fought, Virus!<br /> <br /> Virus had managed to download his mind into Xin's minicomp before his physical body had been dispatched. He didn't wake up until Xin began his clandestine activities, and then quietly waited for an opportunity to start messing with the team.<br /> <br /> One psychic/technokinetic battle later, Miguel successfully manages to shove Virus out of the Askani's computer systems into a physical form that Xin can grab and knock out. Miguel then informs the Askani's staff that he just flushed an Aberrant out of their computers, and finishes his drink while their jaws drop.<br /> <br /> Final GM Notes: I was a bad GM this week, and had nothing prepared outside of the confrontation with Legba. Absolutely everything that came after that was BS'd off the top of my head. I am still amazed that I managed to stretch it out over three hours. But, this week I am overly prepared! I'm so prepared that I gave my players a choice in their next assignment: Bullseye or Scream? Either way, it's going to be a hell of a lot of fun.<br /> <br /> Next Week: The team is given a new assignment, and the mysteries around them begin to deepen. What will they do with this captured Aberrant? Where will they go next? Will their current goals have to be put on hold, or will those goals somehow reach out to find them even in the depths of unexplored space?<br /> <br /> Seriously, I'm asking here. I have no idea! See you next week!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25998-loneshark","name":"Loneshark","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-11T11:04:40-08:00","id":1476219,"title":"","content":"Desperate Stars #3: Operation Seth<br /> <br /> GM Notes: Ugh, what day is it? Friday already? Let's see what I can remember from last weekend. I actually planned it out, this time! I wrote stuff down! I built plot threads! Let's see how well that turned out for me...<br /> <br /> With the Aberrant cult on Duat handled, and Aeon sending in ships to survey the planet and process/deprogram the cultists, the Askani's job here is done. It's called back to Luna for its next assignment. The Leviathan Rasputin was scheduled to deliver new supplies and crew to Tyrian Station on Bullseye, but suffered last-minute technical difficulties that prevented it from taking off. The Askani has been asked to take on the mission in the Rasputin's place.<br /> <br /> The team experiences some strange visions during the jump back to Luna. Miguel sees himself surrounded by a sea of strange machinery, that flows and bobs like an ocean, and then a wave of it rises up to swallow him. Andy sees the orb she found on Duat in her hands, and images of alien worlds flash around her. Giselle sees the photo of her parents and the strange young man she found in the Aberrant cult's lair, and gets an eerie sense that all of it is familiar. Mason has a vision of the day he was recruited into Option-8, and Alex Cassel is there, is head surrounded by a mysterious halo of light. Xin sees... nothing. Nothing at all.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: I forgot about Xin, sorry.<br /> <br /> While they're taking on the Rasputin's cargo and crew at the Lunar dock, each member of the team goes about their own business.<br /> <br /> Xin stays in the medical bay of the Askani to assist Dr. Nezhno Abidemi (the Askani's chief medical officer) with keeping the Aberrant prisoner known as &quot;Virus&quot; in a medical coma, since they have no other way to contain him (it? No one knows, the body Virus manifested with has no squishy sex organs to speak of). He also takes the opportunity to work up a study of the Aberrant's unique physiology. <br /> <br /> Miguel is still working on the problem of containing an Aberrant in both the long term and short term. He messages one of his old grad school friends, Persephone Jones, who is also a fellow electrokinetic. They'd worked with quantum radiation together in their college days, and he consults her about trying to contain the Aberrant. Persephone, for her part, has great news! She's going to be part of the mission to Bullseye! Together, they quickly construct a cell aboard the Askani that should contain Virus and any other Aberrants they come across.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: Convenient, isn't it?<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Andy takes a few hours for &quot;shore leave&quot;, and after a brief shopping montage, she comes back to her quarters on the Dayspring and starts investigating her orb. She senses some faint psionic activity coming from it, and as if guided by some force she manages to get it to produce one of the holographic images she saw in her vision, a hot and humid alien jungle.<br /> <br /> Giselle walks in just in time to see Andy disappear with the orb.<br /> <br /> Andy quickly realizes that this is not a hologram when a large, saurian-like head emerges from the jungle around her. The creature lunges as her while she frantically tries to reactivate the orb, and then she is instantly teleported back to the Dayspring! Giselle does a psychometric reading on the orb, and gets a sense of incredible age. It was floating around in space for a very, very long time before it ended up on Duat. They decide it's best to put the orb aside for now, until their new mission is resolved. Can't have team members disappearing to the wrong alien world, after all.<br /> <br /> Giselle, for her part, spends her personal time attempting to investigate the photo she found. One, who the hell takes paper photos anymore? She does a search on the boy in the picture, but turns up nothing in any database. She even taps one of her contacts, who can't find anything. Still, there is something about the picture that nags at Giselle. She attempts a psychometric reading. What she gets is a reflection of one of her own memories of her parents and herself when she was young, strolling through a street market on a sunny day, just like in the photo. But, when she looks into a mirror in the vision, she sees the boy. Suffice to say that Giselle has no idea what the hell is going on. Every psionic power she uses on the photo sends back the same message: the image is of herself.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Mason volunteers to help oversee the transfer of crew and supplies from the Rasputin. He sees one of the new security personnel (Clara Rosenger, Proteus marine) headed for Bullseye stumble while carrying a small crate of munitions. The Askani's security crew scramble to help her, and in the bustle she surreptitiously slides one of the munitions under another crate. Alarm bells go off in Mason's head, but he plays it cool, and waits until the would-be saboteur has walked away before he retrieves the device. He easily recognizes that it's been rigged with a remote detonator, which he easily disables.<br /> <br /> He takes the device to the Askani's senior crew and his team. The Askani's crew want to arrest the saboteur immediately, but Mason convinces them that they should wait. She will try to detonate the bomb, and when she does, they can trace the signal back to her and both catch her in the act, and see if she has any accomplices. Miguel tracks the detonator's signal when the saboteur attempts to trigger the explosive. The Askani's security team quietly picks her up. Giselle does a psychometric reading of both the detonator and the saboteur, and sees that she was recruited by another woman posing as an officer (Adria Arjona, communications). She also saw a mysterious orb of light floating behind Adria.<br /> <br /> The team waited for Adria to make her move, knowing that she would have to take action when she realized that the bomb failed and Clara was most likely in custody. They watched Adria until she activated a biotech device, which awakened a sleeper agent in Engineering (again, the signal was followed by Miguel). They stopped the second sleeper and apprehended Adria. The Askani's EXO pulled the identities of the remaining sleeper agents from Adria's mind and had them rounded up, while the team questioned Adria.<br /> <br /> Adria said little, other than spouting propaganda and swearing that the Quiet Path would stop the psions from threatening Earth with their reckless exploration. The team jotted down the name, sure that they would run into them again later down the road.<br /> <br /> Now that the Askani was safe, they could make the jump to Bullseye. The team joined the transfer of crew and equipment down to Tyrian Station, being asked to accompany several artifacts found on the planet back up to the ship, where it's hoped that the clairsentients could finally get accurate readings from them. Miguel's curiosity about the Quantum interference on the planet was immediately piqued. Andy tried to use her Upeo membership to get a meeting with the station's manager, Akina Mokoena, but decided to wait until the transfer of supplies was complete.<br /> <br /> The group ran into Dr. Caroline May, who turned out to be a pleasant old woman with a dry sense of humor. She showed them around the core of the station, even down to the labs where some of the artifacts recovered from the planet were being kept. There Miguel was introduced to quad locks, and he immediately started forming plans to reverse engineer them. The team also saw some of the artifacts they were going to bring back to the ship, including the remains of a Bullseye native, and a Grub suit. Their tour was briefly interrupted by Dr. Mataeo Sanhueza, one of the top scientists on the planet, who objected strongly to interference from the psi orders in what he considered a matter of &quot;true science.&quot; Dr. May brushed him off, and he stormed away in a huff.<br /> <br /> Dr. May finally excused herself as the glass storm outside of the habitat died down, and the crew was free to explore as they pleased.<br /> <br /> To Be Continued...<br /> <br /> GM Notes: That was a lot crammed into just three hours for my group, and I didn't even get to the adventures they're going to have on the planet, yet. I'm looking forward to what comes next. In case it's not obvious yet, I'm borrowing heavily from Distant Worlds for plot hooks. We'll see how my group does trying to uncover Bullseye's mysteries, and who or what might try to interfere...<br /> <br /> Next Week: The team will get the chance to see what happened to Bullseye with their own eyes, and participate in a field mission on the planet. Mysterious ruins, vicious aliens, and a warning from the past. See you next week!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25998-loneshark","name":"Loneshark","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-20T13:54:33-07:00","id":1476790,"title":"","content":"GM Notes: Well, last week didn't happen. Xin's player had the sniffles, and I was enjoying my local St. Patrick's Day Festival too much to properly run a game. But this week did happen, and so I was able to run another great session. And here it is!<br /> <br /> Desperate Stars: Operation Seth, Part 2<br /> <br /> At Miguel's insistence, the team sets out to visit the archeological dig site where the quantum manacles were found. Along the way they see the sites that Bullseye has to offer: purple-orange tundra and sky, broken up by glassy ruins and the occasional impact crater. They spot a few animals, which immediately pique Miguel's interest.<br /> <br /> At the dig site they consult with one of the archeologists, Stevie Hunter, who explains the working theory that the place used to be a prison. Giselle, although knowing it might not work, attempts a psychometric reading of the place. She gets only hazy images, unable to break through the planet's psychic static. Miguel gets more general info on the manacles, and the team learns that at least some of the Bullseye natives possessed quantum powers. Were the natives, they wonder, destroyed by their own versions of Aberrants?<br /> <br /> There's some commotion from the other side of the dig, when a bruised and bloody pair emerge from the other side of a ridge. They were part of a survey team that was checking out another potential dig site, when half of their team was ambushed by a &quot;house-keeper&quot;, the largest predators on Bullseye.<br /> <br /> The team sets off to see if they can find the missing surveyors, or at least recover their remains. They are first stalked by a a group of pack-hunting animals. Mason easily scatters them with some laser fire. The team then reaches the ruins, a cluster of crystal buildings surrounded by sand. It's easy to find the survey team's tracks.<br /> <br /> When the team reaches the building were the surveyors' tracks disappear, the house-cleaner immediately strikes, lunging out of the open doorway. Xin steps forward, touches the beast's mandibles, and it instantly turns to dust.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: Hey, Devs, can you nerf Vitakinesis? j/k... sorta.<br /> <br /> Inside, they find the remains of the survey team. Suddenly, Miguel and Andy are drawn to a nearby console of some kind that activated in their presence. They combine their powers, and when Miguel interfaces with the console, a 3-D projection of the planet as it was roughly 200,000 years ago appears above the team's heads. Andy's ability helps the computer pinpoint a signal that it's receiving, and its origin point--somewhere in the deepest part of what remains of Bullseye's oceans.<br /> <br /> With this exciting discovery in hand, they rush back to Tyrian Station, only pausing to yell at Miguel for wanting to capture and vivisect a native pack hunter for study and biological exploitation.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: This actually takes up half-an-hour of real time. Our group is used to having to yell at Miguel's player whenever he tries to enact some hair-brained scheme of dubious legality and utterly lacking in morality. Engineers. Sheesh.<br /> <br /> Andy finally gets a chance to check in with Akina Mokoena. She finds a dispirited and overworked administrator who'd rather be somewhere else, literally. She's rather blase when Andy presents the team's discovery. She ok's a mission, but insists that representatives from each research team on the base also be present, including Dr.s May and Sanhueza. The team hesitantly agrees.<br /> <br /> They accompany artifacts back up to the Askani in the hopes that Giselle will be able to get a better reading on them. Miguel presents one of the xeno-linguists on-board with data copied from the native console in the hopes that she can translate it. Mason is visited by his old boss from Option-8, who warns Mason that not everything on Bullseye, or everyone, is what it seems to be.<br /> <br /> To Be Continued...<br /> <br /> GM Notes: We got started late, and ended late, so everyone was pretty tired by that point and we called it. Dissuading Miguel's player from engaging in exotic species smuggling took up way more time than it should have--I probably should have done more to nip it in the bud, but I don't like to weigh in on the players' intra-party decision making. Mostly because sometimes the crazy shit they come up with on their own entertains me way more than it should.<br /> <br /> No update next week, while I take my daughter to visit her grandparents for Spring Break. I'll be back in April!<br /> <br /> In Two Weeks: A call from the deep, secret agendas, and the team comes face to face... with Death! See ya later!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25998-loneshark","name":"Loneshark","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-05T10:42:54-07:00","id":1478070,"title":"","content":"Hello, everyone! I managed to have a successful game, despite forgetting large amounts of things I told the players the session before! I'm not gonna lie, it wasn't one of my better moments as a GM. But, that was a minor hiccup, fortunately. Andy and Miguel's players had to be NPC'd this session (sick kid and a broken down work truck), so it was just Giselle, Xin and Mason. I'll have to fill the others in next week. Now, without further ado...<br /> <br /> Desperate Stars: Operation Seth, the Conclusion<br /> <br /> Giselle performed her psychometric reading on the Bullseye artifacts brought up to the Askani, getting the full detail on the natives and the invaders. She was assisted in this by Aqua, a Qin telepath, who used Network to share her visions with several gathered scientists. Xin's patron, Dr. Xi, offered to assist, but Giselle was immediately put off by him and refused.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: A little bit of meta-gaming on Giselle's part here, but I allowed it because it didn't interrupt the flow of the game, and allowed me to introduce an NPC that will be recurring later.<br /> <br /> They saw much of the history of the Bullseye natives, including their mastery of Quantum powers, through its mummified remains. They also saw the war that destroyed them firsthand. From the remains of one of the invaders, Giselle saw a mysterious orb that whipped the race up into a frenzy that resulted in Bullseyes' invasion and destruction. The gathered scientists were deeply disturbed by all of these revelations.<br /> <br /> Giselle now had a noetic connection to the &quot;mysterious orb&quot;. She used Dowsing to track it down to Tyrian Station, where it managed to evade her at every turn. Least Resistance suggested that she go ahead and board the Dayspring for its mission to investigate the signal coming from under Bullseye's last remaining ocean.<br /> <br /> They then went down beneath the Bullseye ocean, discovering some interesting but largely harmless native sea life that survived the war. They followed the signal to an underwater complex that was still mostly active. They successfully entered, and found the complex ghostly quiet. Andy and Miguel stayed aboard the Dayspring because of a sudden random malfunction.<br /> <br /> Gm Notes: I was originally going to have an exciting undersea battle with aquatic alien life, but there was little point without the entire group present. Sigh. There's always the next planet...<br /> <br /> Xin, Mason, and Giselle lead the scientific team into the station. They insist that no one should wander off. One of them, Stevie Hunter, notes that the construction is close enough to human norms that the complex can be navigated and a central computer bank could be found to bring the complex under control.<br /> <br /> Giselle and Mason immediately set off on their own while Xin led the science team. Giselle and Mason find their way to a common area in the complex, where they discover the slowly decaying bodies of several Bullseye natives. It looks like they died fighting, but wether it was each other or something else is unclear. Mason finds a computer console and splices enough wiring together to activate a recording. It shows the Bullseye natives, alive, and under attack even as they try to leave a message. What they were under attack from is not clear.<br /> <br /> Then, Giselle gets a premonition, and they manage to escape the compartment before it seals itself off and floods. They radio Xin to let him know that something strange is going on.<br /> <br /> Xin and the science team find the complex's main control room, where they discover several dead Bullseye natives. Xin and the biologists carefully move the bodies while Hunter and the tech specialists get to work. Xi Networks with Miguel, who remotely assists via his Electrokinesis.<br /> <br /> They manage to bring up a holo-display that shows the complex, as well as all life signs currently in it. It also shows that parts of the complex have been systematically flooded to preserve atmosphere and structural integrity, but the process sped up once the team entered. Sanhueza and the Andean Federation contingent have slipped away, and so has Persephone Jones. They manage to pinpoint the Andean Federation, and see that they are disappearing one by one.<br /> <br /> Giselle and Mason, still following Giselle's connection to the orb, run right into a member of the Andean Federation running for his life. He sputters out a warning that Sanhueza has lost his mind and is killing everyone.<br /> <br /> At that dramatically appropriate moment, Sanheuza appears. Giselle can see the orb entity possessing him, the same one that destroyed Bullseye. He tells them that they won't leave the complex alive, and the walls start to groan.<br /> <br /> Giselle and Mason hit him with the psi-dampener rounds, but all they do his slow him down. Xin has Hunter close a blast wall between them, but Sanhueza burns right through it. He attempts (and fails) to possess Mason. Persephone Jones joins the fight, hitting him with a psi power that the team has never seen before. She tells Mason to hit him as hard as he possibly can.<br /> <br /> Mason pushes his powers as he never has before, and turns the hallway into a Hellstorm. At the same time, Miguel channels an energy burst through the building's conduits that zap Sanheuza. The orb rises out of Sanhueza and screams--its death throes are felt by everyone present.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: Did I mention that Miguel's player created psi-dampening rounds? That's just one of his inventions. He won't stop!<br /> <br /> Battered but victorious, the team regroups and gets everyone back to the Dayspring. They return the scientists to Tyrian Station. Xin insists on treating Persephone Jones as an enemy, despite Giselle's insistence otherwise. Persephone refuses to tell them who or what she really is. She instead warns them that the orb creature was called a Doyen. They have been manipulating Humanity and the psions. If the Doyen discover that the team knows about them, they will stop at nothing to destroy them. Then she is teleported away.<br /> <br /> GM Notes: Thus concludes the saga of the team's exploration of Bullseye! I had to GM fiat Persphone's exit a little--Xin's player is paranoid to a fault. But they just uncovered a major setting secret, one which they'll get to revisit later. They now suspect that the Doyen have been actively manipulating the proxies, and are uncertain about who to trust. Just wait until the Chromatics show up...<br /> <br /> Next Week: The start of a new arc! They say in space, no one can hear you scream. That's not strictly true if the scream is an entire planet...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25998-loneshark","name":"Loneshark","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-18T17:18:17-07:00","id":1481417,"title":"","content":"We're Back! Thought I was dead, didn't you? Or wished. Whatever. No such luck for you. People's schedules being what they are these days, my group took a forced hiatus for a few weeks (and another one this week, but that's a story for another time).<br /> <br /> So, yeah. A lot happened recently. Let me bring you up to speed.<br /> <br /> Desperate Stars: Operation Hephaestus, Part 1<br /> <br /> Our heroes (and the term is more apt for some than others) returned Dr. Sanhueza and the remaining members of the Bullseye expedition to Tyrian Station, and joined back up with the Askani to leave. They didn't get much more than a moment's rest before the Askani received an urgent message from Aeon, and off they went on their next mission!<br /> <br /> Twenty six hours previously, a pair of independent psions that go by the call signs of Carnival and Bleak broke the quarantine around Scream. The team was given a bit more background about the planet than what had been leaked and popularized in the public's imagination. Scream is a planet that blasts strange, broken, and random signals into space, aimed roughly at the Creche System, while its surface is overrun with technology. Everything has been converted into machinery or implements of industry, thus making the entire planet into a mad supercomputer. The U.N. had placed a quarantine around it in order to prevent the signals it projects from infecting any Earth ships with their madness.<br /> <br /> How the pair of rogue operatives broke through the quarantine was anyone's guess, but the result was a messy crash landing on the planet's surface. Aeon wasn't willing to risk the Askani to retrieve them, but given the team's history of odds-defying adventures and ability to uncover secrets, they were ordered to try to teleport the Dayspring to the planet, hopefully bypassing the EM &quot;noise&quot; around it, and find Carnival and Bleak for rescue/retrieval/scrape their remains off the pavement.<br /> <br /> (GM Notes: Insert half an hour of Stargate jokes here.)<br /> <br /> Miguel reinforced the Dayspring's systems against EM attacks, Xin ranted angrily about Carnival and Bleak (whose given names are Isabel and Sophia Wong) possibly giving the Great State of China a bad name with their actions, and the rest of the team did what they normally do to prepare for a mission. The Askani took the team as far at the watchpost on the edge of Scream's home system, and after that they were on their own.<br /> <br /> Andy managed to guide the Dayspring into Scream's upper atmosphere, and Miguel's shielded held. (GM Notes: He rolled 9 successes on the shielding the son of a bitch. Andy's player succeeded at her piloting check, but didn't buy off the complication... moohoohaahaah!) Xin, Giselle, and Mason didn't have much else to do but scream and hold on to their butts was Andy brought the Dayspring shakily, but safely down.<br /> <br /> They weren't far from the crash site of Carnival and Bleak's ship; all you had to do was follow the massive furrow it'd left in the steel plating that passed for the planet's &quot;ground&quot;. All around the team, there was tech; grass had been turned into transistors and sparked with electricity, tree leaves spat out and received radio waves like antennae dishes, the rivers and streams were filled with oil and machine coolant, all under an ochre sky. The constant barrage of signals left the team with the hairs on the backs of their necks standing on end--all except for Miguel, of course, who was perfectly comfortable here.<br /> <br /> They found the remains of Carnival and Bleak's ship. The crash had done a number on it, and it definitely wouldn't fly again. A massive hole had been torn into the hull. Carnival and Bleak were nowhere to be seen. Giselle went in and started looking for anything she could psychometrically scan that would give her a connection to the women. Miguel tried talking to the ship's systems, only to find them corrupted by Scream's strange signals. It was by this time that the team realized their minicomps had been corrupted as well. Andy and Mason searched the area for any sign of the women while Xin kept an eye out for life signs with his Kirilian Eye, which at this point had been chillingly absent.<br /> <br /> Mason and Andy found signs of a struggle nearby, and at that moment something approached the group out of the synthetic jungle (GM Notes: This is what happens when you don't buy off your Complications, kids!). Gangly, hunched, too-many-limbs-and-no-faces creatures attacked.<br /> <br /> Mason, who had recently learned of and embraced the joys of Hellscape, froze the entire swarm in their tracks, turning the immediate environment into an arctic wasteland. The machines loved it. The mutants were too dead to care. Then four strangers, humans, emerged from the jungle, thoroughly impressed and politely and calmly asking to talk.<br /> <br /> The four introduced themselves as Sam, Dani, Rhaine, and Robbie. They were scavengers who made a living literally hacking usable parts out of the planet. They were shocked and awed to find strangers from off-planet had managed to safely land here. The team had to restrain Xin from engaging in his default response to meeting new people: paralyzing them, drugging them, and interrogating them, hopefully in that order. They explained that the creatures the team just destroyed with a wave of their hands were sub-aberrant mutants. Aberrants were not liked or welcomed on Scream, but these star-borne strangers seemed to be something else entirely. They suggested that the team should come with them to speak to their mentor and leader, Max Shiir.<br /> <br /> (GM Notes: It was at this point that one of my players FINALLY picked up on my NPC naming convention for every NPC that isn't mentioned in a book. I mean, come on! Did I have to spell it out?!)<br /> <br /> The team decided to split up; Giselle, Andy, and Mason would follow Carnival and Bleak's psychic trail with Sam acting as their guide, while Dani and the others would take Miguel and Xin to Max.<br /> <br /> En route, both parts of the team get similar breakdowns of the planet's current situation. The machines aren't inherently hostile to humans, and will accommodate them if they hold still long enough, but only to provide for basic needs. The mutants they encountered are called Caretakers. Normally they're sedate and go about their business futzing with the planet, but more and more often they've been degenerating into the slavering monstrosities that the team saw. They were created by an Aberrant called Crucible, who then abandoned everyone else on the planet to their fate. Aberrants are hated and either killed or exiled.<br /> <br /> Dani and Miguel bond by her showing him out to work around Scream's interference with their computers. Miguel uses his technokinesis to show her how the planet's source code has been corrupted. Xin expounds upon how the planet will make an excellent colony for the Great State of China. They eventually come to the outskirts of Prosperidad, one of the few cities <br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Sam leads the others through some decidedly more dangerous terrain. They cross a river filled with... something with tentacles, but avoid any serious circumstances (GM Notes: Giselle's player finally made a decent roll on her Survival check to avoid the worst the planet had to throw at them). Back at the crash site, Giselle had successfully learned that Carnival and Bleak had survived, only to be attacked and carried off by rogue Caretakers. After several hours, they finally reach the end of the trail, which is a large, featureless block of a building on a hillside. Sam identifies it as a Caretaker bunker, but it's obviously been corrupted if rogue Caretakers are inside.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Miguel and Xin reach Prosperidad. Insert typical overgrown cyberpunk city description here. Dani and her crew take them to a tower, and up to meet with Max Shiir. They both sense through attunement that Max, to their surprise, has psionic powers just as they do! Max is equally surprised. He introduces the remainder of his students, and connects with Miguel and Xin telepathically so that they may speak candidly. Max explains that he was born with his powers, which include telepathy and electrokinesis, but he has hidden them for fear that he would be mistaken as an Aberrant. He also reveals that one of his students, Warrick, is an alien, a member of a cybernetic race of technopaths who happened upon this planet and was unable to leave because of Scream's disruptions.<br /> <br /> Max goes over the history of the planet, and of how the Aberrant called Crucible led his followers here when it was still an organic paradise. Crucible discovered the terraforming engines inside the planet and activate them. He then warped much of the population into Caretakers (Max's own grandfather among them) and then disappeared. The colony has fought to survive since then, but their numbers are depleting and he is aware that the planet's machines are succumbing to corruption. Miguel and Xin offer to help him figure out how to stop and if possible reverse the corruption happening to the planet. Thanks to his insight into the planet's code, Miguel comes up with a scheme.<br /> <br /> Back to the other half of the team, Giselle uses her psychic senses to peer inside the Caretaker bunker and finds that, behind many normal Caretakers, there is a nest of rogues who hold Carnival and Bleak captive. The women are bloody, dehydrated and exhausted, but alive. They are surrounded by rogue Caretakers and an Aberrant, whose features Giselle cannot clearly discern. The three combine their powers, and using Giselle's psychonavigation to create a point of effect, Andy teleports the women out while Mason simultaneously unleashes a Hellscape on the mutants and the Aberrant.<br /> <br /> (GM Notes: Yes, I know, technically I should have had a telepath with them so that they could all &quot;see&quot; the target. But if it wasn't clear up to this point, I take the Captain Barbosa approach to GMing; they're more like guidelines than actual rules. I only make my players roll to either heighten the tension or, as one of my players put it, &quot;When he's tired of our shenanigans.&quot; And if it's cool enough, I allow it anyway. Funny enough, this exact maneuver is one that our group uses all the time, no matter what game we're playing, or who is GMing. We somehow manage to work it in. We call it the teleport/firebomb. In this case, it was an icebomb.)<br /> <br /> Carnival and Bleak appear beside the team, and they are carted off back to the Dayspring so that they can meet up with Xin and Miguel in Prosperidad.<br /> <br /> Cut Scene: Deep within the bowels of the Caretaker bunker, rogue mutants are covered in a patina of everlasting frost, frozen down to their cells. But a shadow moves beneath the ice, and then breaks through with a horrible, inhuman roar. It catches the scent of those who tried to kill it. It is angry. It will find them. And they will know terror before they die.<br /> <br /> Next Session: Death in the neon jungle! Can a broken planet be healed? The Crucible awaits...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25998-loneshark","name":"Loneshark","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-31T10:46:49-07:00","id":1482506,"title":"","content":"Desperate Stars: Operation Hephaestus, Part 2<br /> <br /> Giselle, Andy, and Mason rescued Carnival and Bleak from their Aberrant captor, while their new friend Sam located a means of swift transport back to the ship. In this case, Sam had wrangled Scream's equivalent of a wild mechanical bull. The mecha-beast came thrashing through the jungle, and scooped the team up onto its back, with Sam howling for joy the entire time. The team found that the girls were in no condition to answer questions, and knew that they had to get them back to the ship as soon as possible.<br /> <br /> Then, Giselle sensed something closing in on them, fast. The team knew they were being followed, and told Sam to speed it up. Then their mecha-steed slammed into something, and sent all of them flying. The team tumbled to the ground, with Andy being the only one to land on her feet. She immediately secured Carnival and Bleak while Giselle and Mason tried to write themselves--fortunately their armor had absorbed the brunt of their fall.<br /> <br /> A shadow loomed up behind Giselle, and struck.<br /> <br /> (GM Notes: So my group foolishly tried to gaslight me into thinking they'd already made it back to the Dayspring at the beginning of the session. My response was to have the Aberrant dump a large amount of its Corruption pool into spontaneously developing teleportation and striking before the team had a chance to prepare itself. Thanks, game mechanics!)<br /> <br /> Giselle managed to roll away from the Aberrant's writhing, spiked tendrils of inky shadow. (GM Notes: Unfortunately, it failed its attack roll miserably) Mason, master of the laser pistol, hit the thing dead center, pinning it down. Andy opened a portal from her position beside the girls, and struck with her vibrosword across the empty space. She tore away a chunk of the Aberrant's hide, exposing a weak point that Giselle fired her pistol into. The Aberrant fell, dead, faster than it had time to act.<br /> <br /> (GM Notes: To all the other Aeon GMs out there, this single Aberrant shit ain't gonna work against a full team of psions. Hell, even of just PCs. I'm gonna have to start using teams of Aberrants.)<br /> <br /> After the team recovered Sam unharmed, they carried the girls the rest of the way to the Dayspring.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Miguel had used his Ghost ability to reconnect with the Dayspring, using the workarounds Dani had shown him. He used another Ghost to try to connect with the planet's insane terraforming engines themselves. The Ghost did not survive the experience, but before it &quot;died&quot; Miguel was able to absorb enough information that allowed him to suss out the corruption in the planet's code. What he discovered was that Crucible was still &quot;alive&quot; in some sense, bonded with the planet's terraforming engines. His own consciousness had been overwhelmed by the alien technology, but his Corruption flooded the code.<br /> <br /> Miguel had a plan.<br /> <br /> (GM Notes: Great, rubbery Cthulhu, please save me from the mad schemes of my players!)<br /> <br /> Xin, meanwhile, talked out a few recent revelations with Max and Warrick. He devised a plan to keep their true natures secret, lest the Doyen hidden within the psi orders find out about them, especially after Warrick revealed that his race were at war with the Doyen. Warrick was eager not only to make contact with the larger portion of Humanity, but make an entreaty for any assistance they could provide his people. Xin decided that it would be best if the Doyen didn't know that Warrick was around, and sent an encoded message to his Patron to ensure the necessary records were kept hidden.<br /> <br /> The Askani was shocked to receive a message from the crew, but relieved to learn that they had been successful. Miguel laid out his plan to the rest of the team and to Cpt. Thomas and the Askani's senior crew. His team would link their powers to root out Crucible, and forcibly tear him from the terraforming engines, body and mind. Cpt. Thomas ordered: &quot;Go for it.&quot;<br /> <br /> There was some initial debate amongst the team about the feasibility/sanity of the plan. Xin was content to go along with it as Scream represented a potentially massive threat to the rest of the galaxy. Andy and Giselle agreed to go along with it on the condition that China get no credit for it (GM Notes: this was just to annoy Xin's player and his tendency to add: &quot;for the great state of China!&quot; to literally every action). Mason just wanted to see what would happen.<br /> <br /> Giselle climbed into her ARES pod, which boosted her psychonavigation enough that she could follow the Corruption threading through the planet back to its source. She could &quot;see&quot; dots of isolated Aberrants here and there, as well as a large gathering far to the north of Prosperidad. There, she also sensed a presence that the team had encountered before--Azimuth! She didn't let it distract her from her mission, however. She found the Crucible deep down in the planet's mantle, where the terraforming engines had originally been placed.<br /> <br /> From there, Andy warped a singularity bomb (GM Notes: You remember, the ones I foolishly allowed Miguel to reverse engineer?) into the complex right at the base of wherever--and whatever--Crucible had become. Mason then fired a Hellscape through the portal, detonating the bomb and expanding it to the equivalent of a solar flare, which unleashed an EMP pulse that knocked Crucible and the engines out of commission.<br /> <br /> Miguel connected to the planet's code again, and severed Crucible's connection while he was stunned. Xin then, using Giselle's noetic viewpoint to target Crucible, remotely unleashed Morbidity on the defenseless Aberrant. There was a massive psionic aftershock as Crucible was torn from this reality down to the atom.<br /> <br /> (GM Notes: This was crazy but epic as Hell. I made it a group effort, and required a large number of successes, given the difference in scale they had between themselves and, well, an entire planet. So, of course they pulled it off. The magnificent bastards.)<br /> <br /> Scream went silent. No machinery hummed, the tendrils that enwrapped its moons froze, and all lights across the planet went out. The planet's terraforming computer had been, effectively, rebooted. Miguel could see in his mind as the computer came back online, it awaited a new command. Miguel told it to, basically, return everything to its original state. The planet shuddered, and then the machinery receded, leaving organic flora and natural elements in its wake. The process took only a few minutes before the entire planet of Scream and its moons were once again lush, life-giving paradises. This time, the psionic reverberations were not of destruction or discontent, but renewal.<br /> <br /> It was all over before the Dayspring had landed in Prosperidad, where they were met with the biggest celebration that they could imagine. (Player Quote: So it's the end of Return of the Jedi, right?) The Askani was able to send down a ship to join the team and congratulate them, while their diplomats met with the city's leaders. There would be a ton of work going forward, but for the moment everyone was just happy.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Carnival and Bleak had been &quot;secured&quot; in the Dayspring's medical bay. Mason, Andy, and Xin debriefed (re:interrogated) the girls. It didn't take much effort for them to give up the fact that the FSA had given them the ship and resources necessary to break the quarantine around Scream. The FSA wanted them to find out the secret of the planet's technology, while the girls admitted that they just did it for the thrill. All of this was included in the team's official report, sure to give the FSA a black eye when it was made public.<br /> <br /> In the aftermath, the team was given a commendation, a raise, and a chance to take a few days off. Andy went to the beach. Mason claimed salvage rights on Carnival and Bleak's crashed ship, which Miguel and Andy promised to help him rebuild and &quot;upgrade.&quot; Xin and his mentor quietly made sure that Max and Warrick's true natures were kept secret from EVERYONE. Especially the psi orders.<br /> <br /> Despite their massive accomplishment, the team had new questions. What had happened to the Caretakers? How would the planet's Aberrants react? What did Azimuth want here? How were they to find and help Warrick's people? Their time on Scream, it seemed, has only just begun.<br /> <br /> They also made one last suggestion to the mission command: That the name of Scream, since it was no longer appropriate, be changed. To Risa.<br /> <br /> (GM Notes: No, really. Risa. Anyway, that was the epic ending to the session. Part of the group is going to be on vacation, so there won't be another session for a couple of weeks. But what a way to end the arc, huh?! My players are crazy, really. But it was a lot of fun. They've been given carte blanche to decide where they go from here, and Giselle's player made the case that she wants to track down what Azimuth was doing on the planet, which would get her closer to her character's long-term goal. So we'll likely see more shenanigans with Aberrants before this is all said and done.)<br /> <br /> Next Time: Risa (yeah, it's sticking) is now a paradise once again, but it's far from pure. What awaits the team when they begin their investigation into the Aberrant activities on the planet? One man's hero is another Aberrant's invader. See you in two weeks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25998-loneshark","name":"Loneshark","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}